00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:16.460
from Michael.
So, while Michael is off, let's continue

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:23.040
exploring Postgres behavior
with DBLab Forge. And today,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:29.060
we have a topic We were discussing
with Kirk, behind the scene, I think,

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.720
- for a couple of years already, right?
- Yeah. Three or more.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.560
Nik: Three or more. [laughs] Yeah. We...
So, Kirk, tell, tell us, your story.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.700
You moved, your system from Oracle, right?

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.260
And this provoked you to use
temporary tables. Am I right?

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:49.880
Kirk: Yes, absolutely.
And after doing it, I met you.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.720
[laughs] And you're like, "No,
don't use temporary tables."

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.940
But we, we struggled because
Oracle has global temporary tables,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.440
and you get it in the global dictionary.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.680
There's no pg_catalog changes
for every session that needs them.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.000
All of that kind of comes along free.
They just have a flag that says, "Oh,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.880
store this in his own files,"
like not with everything else.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.740
So it was so easy to use, and the code
could count on it being there.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.540
It was wonderful.
And when we switched over to Postgres,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.840
we find out, no, we're gonna have
to create these temporary tables

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.940
all the time, and this adds overhead.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.760
And this is our most heavily used search,
is we store results in a temporary table,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.280
and then we fill up extra data in them,
and then we provide them back.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:42.400
And it took us a while to get
this really wired up efficiently,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.490
and to realize that if it's set...
If certain settings are too low,

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.140
you end up spilling over to disk while
you're writing to your temp table.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.600
During practice and running it by hand,
I ran into an issue where it slowed down

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.940
more, and more, and more,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.620
and I realized I was using delete
and I should've been truncating.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.600
- [laughs]
- And you assumed that-

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.500
Kirk: At one point, I actually had to

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.900
- vacuum analyze my temp table-
- Yeah

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.782
Kirk: to get the results
I'm expecting for speed.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.680
- Right
- I'm sorry, what was your question?

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.740
Nik: Yeah. I say, I say maybe truncate
is not, also a good thing in this context,

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.540
- as we will discuss a little bit later.
- Why?

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:26.540
Nik: But I must admit, until this...
Like, until recently, I was, talk-

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.840
I was saying things like
never use sub-transactions,

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.420
never use temporary tables. Well,
I speak from practice. And you can...

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:41.500
It means, like, you can use them if you
have very low loads, right? If you, like,

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.940
have tiny workloads, like below,
like few hundred transactions per second,

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:52.460
for example. It's like you won't notice
big, big, problems. But when you grow,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.940
these things quickly become performance
cliffs, right? Or walls. Cliffs or walls?

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.920
- Cliffs, right? Because you're-
- Yeah, cliffs. You fall off of them.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:02.700
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.140
- Yeah.
- You unexpectedly trip and fall off

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.660
- of them, so...
- Right.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.940
- Right?
- Yeah. And so it can cause even downtime.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.240
So until recently,
I always said this keeping in mind that

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:23.000
temporary tables are bad
in two areas for me. First is

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:28.520
they're actually really hard to diagnose
because you don't see them. Transaction-

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.680
- Mm
- session which created it does see it.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.340
But if, if you're a DBA, like,
trying to... or you have an agent trying

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.811
to diagnose things, connecting to database
in a different session obviously,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.460
you're limited in terms of understanding
what's happening. You, you...

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.880
Like, it's, it's hard.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:51.390
And second thing is second thing is bloat.
You are right. Like, it's just...

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.090
But not bloat of temporary tables.
We need to distinguish things here.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:58.890
If you use a lot of deletes, Autovacuum
will ignore temporary tables, right?

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.380
It, it will just...

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.920
Autovacuum doesn't care about your poor
temporary tables. If you use deletes,

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:13.040
your bloat, content, like,
as, as we all know, MVCC in Postgres-

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.960
- Yep
- deletes, delete is, is done very fast

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:23.420
because it's only marking tuple
as deleted in this session,

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.800
which probably will be, like,
not so if rollback happens, right?

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:34.400
But then, the real, clean,
cleanup happens in, in the vacuum.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.240
Like, Autovacuum runs cleanup.
Everything is great.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.940
But if you delete in your table,
temporary table, Autovacuum doesn't care.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.760
It means you just accumulate
a lot of dead tuples.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.760
It's not bloat, it's dead tuples actually.
I, I, I tend to distinguish,

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.540
dead tuples from bloat
because in my def...

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:58.069
There's no, official definition of bloat.
You cannot find it in documentation

00:05:58.596 --> 00:06:03.276
So different people mean different
things when they say bloat.

00:06:03.276 --> 00:06:08.036
But to me, bloat is what's left
after cleaning up, dead tuples.

00:06:08.036 --> 00:06:10.536
It's empty space which is unused.

00:06:11.056 --> 00:06:16.256
It will be probably used, but in many
cases it will be unlikely used because,

00:06:16.256 --> 00:06:20.616
for example, 90% of our space is empty,

00:06:21.276 --> 00:06:25.516
and nobody will come back
and use it at all. Anyway...

00:06:25.516 --> 00:06:29.575
And yeah, and if you have index
on temporary table, it also degrades.

00:06:29.836 --> 00:06:33.316
This touches our topic
a couple of weeks ago, right?

00:06:33.316 --> 00:06:40.316
int8 versus UUID version
four and version seven, and how, like,

00:06:40.496 --> 00:06:44.516
B-tree can degrade and so on over time.
And in this case, nobody will use it

00:06:44.516 --> 00:06:48.956
probably at all if it's UUID version
seven, so you'll just delete something.

00:06:48.956 --> 00:06:49.956
- What?
- Yeah.

00:06:50.156 --> 00:06:52.816
Kirk: So, so the challenge is, again,

00:06:52.816 --> 00:06:56.116
your advice was rock solid
because it comes from experience.

00:06:56.116 --> 00:07:01.880
And it's hard to put everything that goes
wrong when you use temporary tables into

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:05.430
words, and that's what we're gonna
do in this episode a bit for people,

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.180
is we're gonna make sure you understand
what you're giving up by, by taking

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.856
advantage of this feature.

00:07:10.856 --> 00:07:16.076
And one of the things I wanna point out
is we've tested this now in our system.

00:07:16.316 --> 00:07:21.136
We've rewritten the stored procedures
that do the temporary table work

00:07:21.156 --> 00:07:23.236
and return the data set out of them.

00:07:23.236 --> 00:07:27.556
We've rewritten them to do it all
using in-memory record structures.

00:07:27.616 --> 00:07:31.056
And it's not only faster
to do it this way, okay?

00:07:31.056 --> 00:07:34.456
But the thing is, it's now
a read-only transaction.

00:07:34.456 --> 00:07:38.956
There's no extra transaction being
created because I'm searching.

00:07:39.136 --> 00:07:40.576
So what happens is it's-

00:07:40.576 --> 00:07:43.490
- In other words
- now I can run it on another machine on a

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.930
- read-only server right now. If you use-
- You're jumping to solution

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:48.660
- already,
- Hey, well, well-

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.996
- since you're jumping-
- I'm jumping to the problem, right?

00:07:50.996 --> 00:07:53.030
- Right.
- I hope I'm not jumping to the so yeah,

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.956
- but we found a solution-
- Solution is just-

00:07:54.956 --> 00:07:57.690
- and I only looked for it
- don't use temporary tables.

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:00.130
- And we have-
- Well, yeah, but I just wanted

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:01.410
to let people know that one

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:04.716
way to do it is store it in memory.
Store it in record structure in memory-

00:08:04.716 --> 00:08:07.276
- Yeah
- if you have to. Just use an RTE,

00:08:07.536 --> 00:08:09.536
or there's a handful of ways you

00:08:09.536 --> 00:08:10.676
- can do this.
- Yeah.

00:08:10.676 --> 00:08:13.441
Kirk: It doesn't have
to be overly complicated.

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:16.870
And I think I'll end up writing
an article for the wiki,

00:08:17.376 --> 00:08:21.376
so that in the "don't do this" section,
I'm literally gonna recommend,

00:08:21.376 --> 00:08:23.920
don't use temporary tables,
especially as part-

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.376
- I, I will support this
- of your regular stuff.

00:08:26.376 --> 00:08:32.856
Nik: 100%. So, and, and you use, you said,
these structures, is it in PL/pgSQL code,

00:08:32.856 --> 00:08:37.836
- in, in functions, stored procedures?
- Yeah. Pure PL/pg, you know, SQL. Yeah

00:08:37.836 --> 00:08:41.456
- or just CTE with arrays also will work.
- Well,

00:08:41.456 --> 00:08:47.716
the reason the CTEs don't work for us
is we have, for, for business reasons,

00:08:47.716 --> 00:08:51.076
we actually apply a lot
of business logic to the results.

00:08:51.076 --> 00:08:52.756
So when we bring back results,

00:08:52.756 --> 00:08:55.756
our results aren't like Google
search for something, blah.

00:08:55.756 --> 00:09:00.396
Our results are a lot closer to what
an AI might do, where it then proofs some

00:09:00.396 --> 00:09:06.296
