00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:21.584
- Yeah. Yeah.
- I was done.

00:12:21.584 --> 00:12:24.784
Nik: Let's,
let's untangle this, like, big,

00:12:26.344 --> 00:12:29.544
big thing, of problems
with temporary tables. Okay.

00:12:29.544 --> 00:12:34.444
Problems number zero,
they can be used only on the primary.

00:12:35.304 --> 00:12:37.984
- Yeah. Yeah.
- Problem number

00:12:38.404 --> 00:12:43.344
one for, like, troubleshooting,
you don't see them from other sessions,

00:12:43.344 --> 00:12:47.579
so if something is wrong, it's really
hard to diagnose what's happening.

00:12:47.604 --> 00:12:53.044
Problem number two, 2A and 2B, bloat.

00:12:53.044 --> 00:12:57.064
2A is the bloat itself
if you use delete, but okay-

00:12:57.064 --> 00:12:59.770
- Mm-hmm
- you can just truncate, right? Okay.

00:13:00.423 --> 00:13:05.004
And you need to vacuum, your, on your own,
so it's not, it's not again,

00:13:05.004 --> 00:13:09.004
it's not bloat, dead, dead tuples,
but after vacuum becomes bloat.

00:13:09.004 --> 00:13:13.964
Anyway, the MVCC problems with the content
of these temporary tables themselves.

00:13:14.044 --> 00:13:18.664
But problem 2.2B is a catalog bloat.

00:13:18.864 --> 00:13:20.084
- This is like-
- Yeah

00:13:20.084 --> 00:13:24.804
Nik: for me, like, it's like I saw
it so many times. Yeah. It's like,

00:13:24.804 --> 00:13:31.044
just like you, you come to people and they
have 95, 99%, some catalog tables bloated.

00:13:31.144 --> 00:13:35.024
And as we know, as you know,
you cannot use pg_repack or something.

00:13:35.024 --> 00:13:39.204
You need to vacuum full. Fortunately,
it it's usually, like, just a few seconds.

00:13:39.204 --> 00:13:44.744
Boom, and, and it's done.
Like small surgery. But then other

00:13:45.104 --> 00:13:49.924
queries are, are affected by catalog
bloat because it takes more and more time

00:13:50.044 --> 00:13:53.380
to deal with metadata,
with catalog, right? Sound good?

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.684
Kirk: And two, two guys trying
to create records at the same time.

00:13:56.684 --> 00:14:01.384
Now you have contention. So, and we didn't
see how bad it was until we kind of ran

00:14:01.384 --> 00:14:05.864
a count and were watching
for transaction ID wraparound.

00:14:05.964 --> 00:14:11.044
Thankfully, it gets handled automatically,
but what we thought was 10 million

00:14:11.304 --> 00:14:15.164
queries a day running through,
we didn't realize because we,

00:14:15.164 --> 00:14:19.184
we had two temp tables and an index,
temporary index,

00:14:19.184 --> 00:14:24.264
that those are three records going
in, and they're three records coming out

00:14:24.344 --> 00:14:26.004
when that session's over, right?

00:14:26.004 --> 00:14:29.464
When you free up your temp tables,
then those three have to come out.

00:14:29.464 --> 00:14:34.804
So what was 10 million a day
quickly became 60 million

00:14:34.844 --> 00:14:38.124
- updates to pg_catalog a day.
- Mm-hmm.

00:14:38.124 --> 00:14:42.183
Kirk: Okay? Pretty soon, we're just
wasting time updating pg_catalog.

00:14:42.384 --> 00:14:45.064
Like, you know, for, for no good reason.

00:14:45.064 --> 00:14:46.504
- [laughs]
- Right.

00:14:46.504 --> 00:14:51.224
Kirk: So, and that, and that has
a whole different raft of problems

00:14:51.224 --> 00:14:54.704
if you cross certain barriers, so.

00:14:54.824 --> 00:15:01.504
Nik: Yeah. And our silicon friend here
who is silent because I told, I, I,

00:15:01.504 --> 00:15:04.864
I told him not to speak
unless directly addressed.

00:15:04.864 --> 00:15:09.144
He says also that there is a, like,
interesting hidden issue with

00:15:10.544 --> 00:15:12.784
so transaction ID wraparound.

00:15:13.124 --> 00:15:16.948
I can imagine in very heavily
loaded environments it might happen

00:15:17.004 --> 00:15:21.964
that a vacuum cannot,
take care of, like, cannot freeze, right?

00:15:22.844 --> 00:15:28.044
The, the content of the the tuples
in temporary tables,

00:15:28.044 --> 00:15:29.924
which is also additional risk.

00:15:30.044 --> 00:15:35.944
I would still put it, to category, like,
the, to item two, complex item two, right?

00:15:35.944 --> 00:15:38.744
- Okay.
- It's all about MVCC and bloat and so on

00:15:38.744 --> 00:15:41.584
in that layer.
And finally, layer number three,

00:15:41.584 --> 00:15:45.504
which is central topic for this podcast,
which is very, very interesting.

00:15:45.504 --> 00:15:49.404
It doesn't it's not related
to bloat, MVCC, nothing.

00:15:50.364 --> 00:15:56.404
It's related to how Postgres works, when
something, like, when any DDL happens,

00:15:56.404 --> 00:16:01.504
including, temporary table creation,
and actually truncate as well.

00:16:01.604 --> 00:16:07.884
So it, it looks like a wait event,
lightweight lock,

00:16:08.103 --> 00:16:15.084
SInvalWrite, SInvalRead.
So, a snapshot invalidation,

00:16:15.084 --> 00:16:18.824
catalog snapshot invalidation,
and it happens when one session,

00:16:19.564 --> 00:16:26.564
executes some DDL or truncate as well, and
it signals, that other sessions must also,

00:16:27.424 --> 00:16:31.424
like,
refresh their understanding of catalog.

00:16:31.464 --> 00:16:35.484
And interesting that I was thinking,
like, I, I couldn't understand why

00:16:35.484 --> 00:16:39.544
logically it doesn't make sense to me.
Why, why do we do it?

00:16:39.544 --> 00:16:43.844
Because temporary tables,
they are visible to only to our session.

00:16:44.024 --> 00:16:48.944
Why do we need to signal others
anything at all? What do you think?

00:16:48.944 --> 00:16:51.244
- Right.
- Why? [laughs] Why?

00:16:52.344 --> 00:16:55.684
- Yeah.
- And I... Honestly, it may just be

00:16:55.724 --> 00:17:00.504
a, like a trigger on pg_catalog that says,
"Hey, the catalog's changed.

00:17:00.504 --> 00:17:04.020
Tell everyone that their catalog
assumptions might be wrong,"

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:07.624
right? That the optimizer might have
to look at maybe it added a column,

00:17:07.624 --> 00:17:12.684
maybe it did this. So maybe it
was an overly safe approach to, hey,

00:17:12.684 --> 00:17:17.264
catalog change, just send this out, okay?
So that it gets handled. Because you're

00:17:17.264 --> 00:17:23.444
dead on. There's only one backend that can
truly read your temp tables, right?

00:17:23.444 --> 00:17:26.184
And needs to know about
changes to your temp tables.

00:17:26.184 --> 00:17:28.584
The problem is, it's a global catalog.

00:17:28.784 --> 00:17:34.464
Nik: Right. Right. It is global catalog.
Exactly. Data is, is local.

00:17:34.464 --> 00:17:38.084
A catalog is, is global.
This is just mechanism Postgres uses.

00:17:38.084 --> 00:17:44.704
And and one of the ideas, DBLab Forge
brought, as usually we have ideas for

00:17:44.764 --> 00:17:49.324
current versions, for operations,
for DBREs and their agents

00:17:49.748 --> 00:17:54.528
And additionally, separately, we have
ideas for future improvements in Postgres.

00:17:54.588 --> 00:17:58.768
And one of the ideas to make it
truly local, but it, it's like,

00:17:58.768 --> 00:18:00.668
I guess it's just hard because

00:18:01.448 --> 00:18:05.148
because how work with catalog
is organized in Postgres.

00:18:05.348 --> 00:18:09.588
There, there was work on implementing
global temporary tables for guys who

00:18:09.588 --> 00:18:15.768
are moving from Oracle should be good. But
I think it's abandoned a few years ago,

00:18:15.948 --> 00:18:21.728
so I'm not sure about the status of that.
Anyway, moving back to this thing.

00:18:21.748 --> 00:18:28.408
So every time, you create a, a temporary
table or or destroy it or truncate it,

00:18:28.568 --> 00:18:31.948
this signal goes to this queue,
invalidation queue,

00:18:32.308 --> 00:18:38.808
which is 4,096 hard-coded constant, right?

00:18:38.808 --> 00:18:43.288
- Okay.
- Yeah. And what happens, when you,

00:18:44.088 --> 00:18:49.348
as DBLab Forge found, first you don't
see that particular lightweight lock.

00:18:49.528 --> 00:18:52.728
If you take, for example,
Postgres 18 or Postgres 19,

00:18:52.728 --> 00:18:56.508
which is release candidate 2
right now, right? It will...

00:18:56.508 --> 00:19:01.108
And you just start in many sessions,
start, creating temporary tables,

00:19:01.588 --> 00:19:06.868
and increase number of such sessions,
increase contention. On 16-core machine,

00:19:08.688 --> 00:19:10.548
you will start seeing problems. Like,

00:19:10.548 --> 00:19:13.708
first you will start seeing
lightweight lock LockManager,

00:19:13.708 --> 00:19:19.288
which is understandable because if you
have indexes and a lot of relations,

00:19:19.548 --> 00:19:25.108
fast path is not, happening,
is not, being used

00:19:25.148 --> 00:19:31.228
if you exceed the total number
of relations in transaction exceeding 16.

00:19:32.488 --> 00:19:37.248
Which is quite easy to solve,
super easy to solve in Postgres 18,

00:19:37.448 --> 00:19:42.928
just increasing max_locks_per_transaction
setting. Setting it, for example, 128,

00:19:42.928 --> 00:19:48.068
256, and then, this,
this lightweight lock goes away.

00:19:48.468 --> 00:19:53.288
And then you see this lightweight
lock SInvalWrite.

00:19:53.967 --> 00:20:00.448
And the interesting thing
is that if you see it, usually

00:20:00.648 --> 00:20:05.668
a CPU is low and disk IO is low,
can be close to zero.

00:20:05.868 --> 00:20:09.728
So you don't see the server as loaded
at all, but you are not getting-

00:20:09.728 --> 00:20:13.248
- Mm
- throughput as you should because

00:20:13.548 --> 00:20:18.548
this is just this, like,
bottleneck, this invalidation queue.

00:20:19.188 --> 00:20:26.088
And the problem further
increases if you have

00:20:26.368 --> 00:20:32.528
additional, idle sessions, which is quite
common on RDS and Aurora. They...

00:20:32.528 --> 00:20:37.708
Like, we see clients all the time coming
to us with, like, 2,500 max connections,

00:20:37.708 --> 00:20:39.548
5,000 max connections.

00:20:39.888 --> 00:20:44.728
And they start, like, when project
is growing, they start using them.

00:20:45.028 --> 00:20:49.048
Even if they use... There is no PgBouncer
usually because you need to manage it,

00:20:49.048 --> 00:20:51.048
- like, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:20:51.048 --> 00:20:53.968
Nik: Amazon doesn't provide
it as a managed service.

00:20:53.968 --> 00:20:58.628
They, they provide RDS proxy,
but RDS proxy is very weird thing.

00:20:58.628 --> 00:21:03.808
We see it's, like,
provoking idle sessions really well.

00:21:03.828 --> 00:21:09.048
We just see a lot of idle sessions, so it
doesn't help to shave off, idle sessions.

00:21:09.048 --> 00:21:12.695
So it's quite common you just have,
like, 2,000 idle sessions.

00:21:13.508 --> 00:21:14.568
- Wow.
- Yeah.

00:21:14.568 --> 00:21:21.568
And this directly negatively affects,
this very problem, this lightweight lock

00:21:21.988 --> 00:21:26.028
SInvalWrite contention,
as you can see from the graphs.

00:21:26.948 --> 00:21:33.948
So, this means that, like, you just have
bottleneck. You, you experience a problem,

00:21:34.068 --> 00:21:38.988
like performance issue. It's performance
cliff. It can come, like, in waves.

00:21:39.848 --> 00:21:44.328
If you have a lot of idle sessions,
it can be, like, constant.

00:21:44.328 --> 00:21:48.868
You're basically already falling
from the cliff and just lie down there

00:21:49.008 --> 00:21:51.728
on the bottom, right? And and it...

00:21:51.728 --> 00:21:58.028
I, I can imagine in some cases it can
be downtime, just like outage, right?

00:21:58.388 --> 00:22:01.988
And if you just...
Like, first urgent emergency help,

00:22:01.988 --> 00:22:08.988
just shave off idle sessions. Find a way,
like, bring PgBouncer or better plan,

00:22:09.488 --> 00:22:15.088
application side, pool connection sizes,
right? You can... Usually you can cut it.

00:22:15.128 --> 00:22:20.168
How it happens, like right now we are,
like, how many weeks, before Black Friday?

00:22:20.748 --> 00:22:23.208
- What usually happens, I saw-
- Three.

00:22:23.208 --> 00:22:30.208
Nik: Yeah. Two or three, yeah. So Black
Friday is when e-commerce companies start,

00:22:32.728 --> 00:22:36.608
bringing more capacity to handle
Black Friday well, because it's like,

00:22:36.608 --> 00:22:41.888
it's like tsunami workload usually for
successful businesses. And they just...

00:22:41.888 --> 00:22:46.608
How it, like, usually works, guys who
are responsible for application, they say,

00:22:46.608 --> 00:22:51.308
"Oh, our application is quite heavy.
Let's double or triple, quadruple

00:22:51.388 --> 00:22:56.108
number of application nodes." But usually
they just forget about database,

00:22:56.108 --> 00:22:58.588
and they just don't talk to database.

00:22:58.588 --> 00:23:04.508
And if there's no PgBouncer, which is,
which protects from extra idle sessions,

00:23:05.348 --> 00:23:08.628
they just, like, say,
"Okay, we just double double

00:23:08.988 --> 00:23:11.788
- number of application nodes."
- Yeah.

00:23:11.788 --> 00:23:16.908
Nik: But they don't, don't... Like, they
have application size poolers, say like,

00:23:16.908 --> 00:23:22.188
40. Like, they have like 100 nodes, 40.
Oh, too much, too many, right?

00:23:22.188 --> 00:23:29.188
Say, okay, 10. 10 too small.
Okay. Say some of all sessions,

00:23:29.388 --> 00:23:35.308
before the their change was 1,000,
and we were okay with it.

00:23:35.308 --> 00:23:41.568
Okay, it was RDS 5,000 max connections,
but application side pooler protected us.

00:23:41.568 --> 00:23:43.408
We didn't exceed 1,000.

00:23:43.408 --> 00:23:47.968
But now they decided to double or,
or triple the number of application nodes,

00:23:48.088 --> 00:23:52.148
not changing the size
of pooler on application side.

00:23:52.704 --> 00:23:56.164
So we just suddenly have more
connections potentially, right?

00:23:56.164 --> 00:23:59.664
- Right.
- Yeah. And the database, okay,

00:23:59.784 --> 00:24:05.704
it handles quite well and after
Postgres 13, 14 it thanks to work

00:24:05.804 --> 00:24:11.690
from Andres Freund, it handles really
well extra idle connections usually.

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:15.544
Like you can couple of thousand idle
connections, you don't suffer from them.

00:24:15.544 --> 00:24:20.944
Except this point, if you intensively
use temporary tables, it will hurt you.

00:24:20.944 --> 00:24:27.284
So this wait event can be seen
in active session history dashboards,

00:24:27.284 --> 00:24:32.684
like Performance Insights
or our dashboards, PostgresAI dashboards.

00:24:32.844 --> 00:24:38.024
Or if you, if you use pg_ash, you can see
it as well. And you just see, oh, boom,

00:24:38.024 --> 00:24:44.124
like a lot of sessions are waiting
in that wait, wait event. CPU is not 100%.

00:24:44.384 --> 00:24:49.104
Disk I/O is far from saturation, maybe
even close to zero if we have very cached

00:24:49.424 --> 00:24:54.784
state as we had in this synthetic case,
by DBLab Forge.

00:24:55.024 --> 00:24:59.924
And nothing is happening, right?
And this actually very classic

00:25:00.024 --> 00:25:06.364
situation of a problem, performance
problem, which Brendan Gregg in his,

00:25:07.104 --> 00:25:11.504
I, I highly recommend, there are a couple
of videos, from some conference,

00:25:12.164 --> 00:25:17.104
where he just walks through various
Linux tools. And one of the cases,

00:25:17.104 --> 00:25:22.204
like CPU is almost zero, disk I/O is zero,
but performance is not there.

00:25:22.204 --> 00:25:26.800
What's happening, right?
This is happening. We're just waiting.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:30.804
Some, some bottleneck, and we're just
waiting for something, and, and that's it.

00:25:30.804 --> 00:25:35.004
Kirk: And again, it's a, it's, it's
a basically a 4K ring buffer that we're

00:25:35.004 --> 00:25:38.780
dropping these messages into,
and we've got to wait till everybody

00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:42.870
basically takes off and says, yeah,
I cleared my... You know, I listened.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:44.870
- I know. Thank you very much."
- Mm-hmm.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:47.650
And the challenge
is this is going out to everybody.

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:49.064
- It's a broadcast.
- Mm-hmm.

00:25:49.064 --> 00:25:53.284
Kirk: And you're, you're now
broadcasting it, not to 1,000 sessions,

00:25:53.284 --> 00:25:56.484
- but now to 4,000 sessions.
- All idle ones. Yeah.

00:25:56.484 --> 00:26:00.743
Kirk: And all idle ones. And you're
broadcasting then to them, "Hey,

00:26:01.083 --> 00:26:04.404
just confirm that you know you
have to update your cache here,

00:26:04.404 --> 00:26:06.698
because somebody created a temp table."

00:26:06.784 --> 00:26:10.444
And the worst part is, is the more
temp table creation you get,

00:26:10.444 --> 00:26:15.164
the more of those messages you get.
And the queue doesn't change in size.

00:26:15.184 --> 00:26:17.604
So now all of a sudden
there's contention there,

00:26:17.604 --> 00:26:22.244
and now you get people waiting and
waiting, and you have to wait to process.

00:26:22.244 --> 00:26:26.384
And all of a sudden now everything
you're doing is just running slower.

00:26:26.384 --> 00:26:30.804
The queries didn't get any harder,
okay? Nothing really...

00:26:30.804 --> 00:26:35.340
It's not the work, it's the information,
right? This is like adding a late,

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:38.540
adding an extra developer to a late
project only makes it later.

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:41.990
This is adding a session to a system
that's overwhelmed by the number

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:47.024
of sessions, only makes the system more
overwhelmed by the number of sessions.

00:26:47.024 --> 00:26:48.590
- Yeah.
- So interesting.

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:50.844
- And then
- You, you had just mentioned the,

00:26:50.844 --> 00:26:52.010
- the graph.
- Yeah.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:56.120
I think it's a good time to mention,
'cause, you know, simply you can rewrite

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:59.500
a lot of these queries like you
did for this as a CTE, right?

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:01.804
- Mm-hmm.
- And we have this graph that the AI

00:27:01.804 --> 00:27:06.184
will bring up or the, you know,
whoever will bring up and show it to you.

00:27:06.184 --> 00:27:09.724
- That it's amazing the, the difference.
- Yeah.

00:27:09.724 --> 00:27:11.444
- It's like-
- 20,000

00:27:11.444 --> 00:27:17.750
Kirk: yeah, 20,000, you know,
versus 80,000 transactions per second.

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:18.890
- Hold on.
- Depending-

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:22.484
I'm looking at the graph,
which is like 88,000 transactions for CTE.

00:27:22.484 --> 00:27:26.164
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah, that one.
- And less than 4,000

00:27:26.444 --> 00:27:29.464
for temporary tables which
are created per transaction.

00:27:29.464 --> 00:27:32.824
So temporary table,
create table statement for temporary,

00:27:32.824 --> 00:27:35.164
- create temporary table statement.
- Yeah.

00:27:35.164 --> 00:27:39.264
Nik: There's modifiers
ON COMMIT DELETE ROWS or ON COMMIT DROP.

00:27:39.744 --> 00:27:43.624
- So for ON COMMIT DROP, it's the worst.
- Yes.

00:27:43.624 --> 00:27:47.264
Nik: Because you are, like you
basically for every transaction you,

00:27:47.264 --> 00:27:49.784
you broadcast more messages, right?

00:27:50.144 --> 00:27:53.900
But for ON COMMIT DELETE ROWS,
this is your case,

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:55.420
- like, which will lead-
- Yeah

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:59.440
to different problem. It will lead to a
lot of dead tuples. You need to vacuum.

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:01.690
- Okay.
- You have now bloat and like indexes

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:07.864
are out of shape. So that is good here
because slight, like good like four,

00:28:07.864 --> 00:28:12.464
four or five times better in terms
of maximum TPS you can achieve.

00:28:12.464 --> 00:28:16.964
So you have 17,000 transactions
in this synthetic experiment.

00:28:17.084 --> 00:28:22.784
Because okay, you are deleting rows.
If we forget about the tuples and bloat,

00:28:23.264 --> 00:28:29.324
then we just avoid extra DDL and extra
broadcast, through that queue, right?

00:28:29.324 --> 00:28:33.544
So this is like slightly better,
but I would, I wouldn't still

00:28:33.644 --> 00:28:36.444
use temporary tables at all.

00:28:36.444 --> 00:28:43.024
Kirk: Well, and, and the CTE version
is like more than four times better

00:28:43.024 --> 00:28:48.144
than that. You're getting 80 some
thousand rows or transactions per second.

00:28:48.144 --> 00:28:53.424
Like it's... When you see the graph,
that should be what's telling you, oh,

00:28:53.484 --> 00:28:57.830
I probably don't want a system that's
heavily dependent on temporary tables.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:01.084
Now look, they have their place.
If it's a once-a-month batch job

00:29:01.084 --> 00:29:04.944
or something like that, or it can't
fit in the memory, it's insane,

00:29:04.944 --> 00:29:07.664
and you have to do some stuff,
that's perfect.

00:29:07.664 --> 00:29:12.524
Like, do that. But don't use it
as part of every search we do.

00:29:12.524 --> 00:29:15.484
[laughs] As our system
grows and we do more,

00:29:15.764 --> 00:29:18.564
this is just gonna
compound on temp tables.

00:29:18.684 --> 00:29:21.344
Again, had I do-
had to do it all over again,

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:25.504
we would've not implemented what we did,
so... And we will change it. [laughs]

00:29:25.504 --> 00:29:29.144
Nik: I, I know only one case
where temp tables are needed,

00:29:29.144 --> 00:29:32.164
is guys migrating
from Oracle really want them.

00:29:32.164 --> 00:29:35.364
Beyond that, I,
I cannot imagine any case where, where,

00:29:35.364 --> 00:29:39.104
where I would personally use
temp tables at all. Zero.

00:29:39.724 --> 00:29:43.424
Kirk: Zero. Even if, like for example,
you're asked to do a bunch of

00:29:43.424 --> 00:29:47.524
data analysis, and you have to take
the data and massage it into a different

00:29:47.524 --> 00:29:52.304
structure so that you can then load it
into what effectively is what management

00:29:52.304 --> 00:29:53.630
- wants. Because-
- Mm-hmm

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:55.490
it's being significantly pivoted,

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:57.664
- et cetera, and they're gonna look at-
- Well

00:29:57.664 --> 00:30:00.546
Kirk: and spend time
querying it over next month.

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:06.900
Nik: F-follow me, like, and, and again,
YouTube guys, watching us on YouTube will,

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:12.240
will see it in the center of the screen
right now. So imagine you have a table,

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:17.560
and you select from it. Select star
from this table, right? Or select...

